// A String reached out of a parsed tree, through a `match` payload and a `let`, is still live when
// the block ends.
//
// **This was a use-after-free that ANSWERED rather than crashed**, which is the failure this
// language exists to refuse. The same expression read twice gave `hello there` and then `l`. In a
// longer chain the length prefix was overwritten too, so a program read a plausible String of the
// WRONG length and carried on — one report had `len` of 38 for an eleven-character string, where 38
// was the length of the previous print statement's own text. A third face was the region reporting
// itself exhausted, because the allocator's bookkeeping had been overwritten.
//
// The cause was one hop in `collect_relayed_sources`. It walks `Field`, `Index`, `Try`, `StructLit`
// and `VariantLit`, but its `Var` arm resolved only PARAMETERS — so a name introduced by a pattern
// is not a parameter, the walk stopped, and `json_as_text` was never recorded as relaying its
// argument. `ReleasePass::allocates` then answered NO for a call to it, `store` did not taint the
// frame, the frame was judged to keep nothing, and the `Release` freed the tree the returned String
// still pointed into.
//
// **The rule is not "a pattern binding relays" — it is "a name bound to relayed storage relays".**
// `json_at` needs both hops in one function: `Object(fields)` is a pattern binding and
// `let f: Field = fields[i]` is a `let`. Teaching only the pattern moved the failure one statement
// later, which would have looked like a fix.
//
// **It uses the real library on purpose.** Three synthetic versions of this fixture passed against
// the broken compiler before this one — the shape needs a tree allocated inside the function and a
// value relayed out of it through two different kinds of binding, and each simplification lost the
// part that mattered. A fixture that cannot fail against the bug it names is worse than none.
//
// Two reads on purpose. One read cannot find this: the first read is correct, and it is the
// allocation the first read performs that lands on the freed storage.
use "../../lib/json.bx";

class Holder { text: String }

function pull(source: String) -> Holder {
    let mutable found: String = "";
    match json_parse(source) {
        Error(e) => { }
        Ok(v) => {
            match json_at(v, "name") {
                None => { }
                Some(field) => {
                    match json_as_text(field) {
                        None => { }
                        Some(t) => { found = t; }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    return Holder { text: found };
}

region main {
    let h: Holder = pull("\{\"name\":\"hello there\"\}");
    // CONCATENATION, not interpolation, and that is load-bearing: it is the allocation the first
    // print performs that lands on the freed storage. An interpolated version of this fixture
    // passed against the broken compiler.
    print("first  len " + to_string(len(h.text)) + " = [" + h.text + "]");
    print("second len " + to_string(len(h.text)) + " = [" + h.text + "]");
}
